.TH RUN-DIGEST 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
run-digest \- the deterministic daily digest and Monday rollup: ingest, enrich, ticket, brief, send
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B run\-digest
.RB [ daily | weekly ]
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run | \-\-for\-real | \-\-to\-dev )
.RB [ \-\-lookback
.IR DURATION ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent run\-digest
.RB [ daily | weekly ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run-digest
builds the CTI digest and mails it. It is plain shell with no model in the
loop, so delivery never depends on the orchestrator noticing the clock.
.PP
It is run by two units:
.TP
.B cti\-agent\-digest.service
.B run\-digest daily
from
.BR cti\-agent\-digest.timer ,
every day at 06:00
.RB ( Persistent=true :
a missed run fires at boot).
.TP
.B cti\-agent\-weekly.service
.B run\-digest weekly
from
.BR cti\-agent\-weekly.timer ,
every Monday at 07:00
.RB ( Persistent=true ).
.PP
The installer replaces both times with
.B FLEET_DIGEST_TIME
and
.B FLEET_WEEKLY_TIME
in a drop-in when
.B FLEET_TIMEZONE
is set. Both units run as
.B ctiagent
with
.BR UMask=0077 ,
and a failure triggers
.BR cti\-agent\-alert@.service .
.PP
The steps, in order:
.TP
.B 0. Already sent?
On a real run,
.B fleet\-db was\-sent
.I DATE KIND
is asked whether this kind of digest already went out today (local date).
If it did, the runner says so and exits 0. If
.B fleet\-db
fails, the digest is treated as not sent. A dry run skips the check.
.TP
.B 1a. Ingest (daily)
Runs the Go ingest binary from
.BR CTI_AGENT_DIR :
.I $CTI_AGENT_DIR/cti\-agent
if it is executable, otherwise
.B go run ./cmd/cti\-agent
in that directory, with
.B REPORT_PATH
set to today's raw report and
.B \-\-lookback
passed through when given. It reads the CTI mailbox over Graph, looks the
CVEs up in the scanner, writes
.IR reports/raw\-DATE.md ,
and records the messages it read in the processed-message log that
.BR run\-mailbox\-cleanup (1)
relies on. Fatal if
.B CTI_AGENT_DIR
is unset or missing, if the binary exits non-zero, or if the raw report is
empty.
.TP
.B 1b. Rollup (weekly)
The weekly does not read the mailbox. It runs
.B fleet\-db rollup \-\-days
.I N
.RB ( WEEKLY_DAYS ,
default 7) over the stored findings into
.IR state/weekly\-enriched\-DATE.json ,
fatal if that fails or is empty. It then copies the newest daily ticket map
.RI ( state/enriched\-DATE\-tickets.json )
beside it, mode 0600, so the weekly can link tickets; with no daily map the
weekly renders without links.
.TP
.B 2. Enrich (daily)
.B bin/cti\-enrich \-\-report
.I raw
.B \-\-out
.IR state/enriched\-DATE.json :
NVD, EPSS and CISA KEV, and an upsert of the findings into
.IR state/memory.db .
Fatal on failure. The weekly skips it; its findings were scored when first
ingested.
.TP
.B 2b. Ticket (daily, non-fatal)
If
.I $FLEET_CODE/bin/cti\-jira
is executable:
.B cti\-jira \-\-from\-enriched
.I file
.B \-\-for\-real \-\-follow\-up
on a real run, the same without
.B \-\-for\-real
on a dry run. Any failure, including the binary being absent, costs tickets
and nothing else. The weekly never runs it.
.TP
.B 2c. Advisory triage (daily, TRIAGE_ENABLED=1, non-fatal)
Only with
.B TRIAGE_ENABLED=1
(0 or 1; anything but 1 is off, and the log says
.BR "triage: off" )
ingest is run with
.B \-\-advisories\-out
and saves every threat-intel message, whoever sent it (one holding a CVE,
ATT&CK ID, defanged indicator or link, public IP or file hash), plus any
from an authenticated
.B TRIAGE_SENDERS
sender, as
.IR state/enriched\-DATE\-advisory\-input.json .
.B cti\-triage \-\-batch
then has the reader list each one's items; items naming a CVE are left to
the CVE report, and the rest go through the Falcon lookups and the judge
(at most
.BR TRIAGE_MAX_PER_RUN ,
20 minutes in all) and writes
.IR state/enriched\-DATE\-advisories.json ,
which
.B cti\-brief
renders as FLEET ADVISORIES. The input is removed once triaged and kept if
triage fails. Runs on a dry run too, in its directory, so the preview is
what the real digest would show; that costs the same model calls. Any
failure costs the section and nothing else.
.TP
.B 3. Brief
.B bin/cti\-brief \-\-daily
(or
.BR \-\-weekly )
renders
.I reports/digest\-KIND\-DATE.html
and the matching
.IR .txt ,
then is run again with
.B \-\-subject\-only
for the subject line. Fatal on failure.
.TP
.B 4. Send
.B bin/cti\-mailer
with the HTML, the text, the subject and
.I reports/raw\-DATE.md
attached, plus
.B \-\-dry\-run
on a dry run. The mailer resolves recipients from
.B DIGEST_TO
and
.B DIGEST_CC
against
.BR VM_ALLOW_TO ;
see the mailer for the send gate. Fatal on failure. The mailer's JSON
result is printed.
.TP
.B 5. Log
On a real run: the Sev5..Sev1 counts from the enriched file (read with
.BR "cti\-brief \-\-counts" )
and the Graph request id (which the mailer writes to
.I state/enriched\-DATE\-request\-id
with
.BR \-\-request\-id\-out )
are recorded with
.BR "fleet\-db sent" ,
which is what step 0 checks tomorrow, and an INFO line is posted to the
board for
.BR @you .
A count that cannot be read is recorded as 0 and a missing request id as
.BR unknown :
the mail has gone, so the record must not fail. The runner runs no Python.
On a dry run only a board line naming the HTML file.
.PP
A fatal step prints
.B FAILED:
and posts an ERROR line to the board before exiting 1.
.PP
A dry run still does everything up to the mailer: the daily reads the
mailbox, calls NVD and the scanner, and runs
.B cti\-jira
in its own dry-run mode. It does not send mail, does not file or comment on
tickets, does not record the digest as sent, and writes none of the real
run's files: not the processed-message log, not
.IR memory.db ,
not today's report, enriched file or digest (see DRY RUNS AND THE LANE
LOCK). The real run that day still goes out, and reports what it would have
reported without the rehearsal.
.PP
.B run-digest
never sends twice for the same day and kind, never reads the mailbox for the
weekly, and never runs
.B cti\-jira
for the weekly.
.SH HOW TO RUN
\fBrun-digest\fR sends the digest and records it, so it has no default: it has to be told how to run. Give exactly one of
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR (do everything but send and record), \fB\-\-for\-real\fR (send and record it),
or \fB\-\-to\-dev\fR (a test send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only; it may be combined with \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR to rehearse the test send).
With none it prints a usage message and exits 2 without doing anything, and
two that contradict each other are refused the same way. This is the rule for
every fleet command that changes something, whether a person, a systemd unit
or the heartbeat runs it, so a unit that lost its flag fails loudly instead of
quietly doing nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
These are shell arguments, matched exactly and accepted in any order. Any
other argument is refused with a usage line and exit status 2.
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
Send the digest and record it. One of this, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR or \fB\-\-to\-dev\fR is required.
.TP
.BR daily ", " weekly
Which digest. Given more than once, the last wins.
Default:\~\fBdaily\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Run every step but send nothing: the mailer gets
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
.B cti\-jira
runs without
.BR \-\-for\-real ,
the already-sent check is skipped and nothing is recorded as sent. See
DESCRIPTION for what a dry run still writes.
.TP
.B \-\-to\-dev
A test send: every step runs as for
.B \-\-dry\-run
(no already-sent check or record,
.B cti\-jira
without
.BR \-\-for\-real ,
no board line), but the digest is mailed through
.B cti\-mailer \-\-to\-dev
to
.B FLEET_DEV_EMAIL
only, subject prefixed
.BR "[DEV TEST]" .
Safe to run after the real digest has gone. With
.B \-\-dry\-run
as well, nothing is mailed.
.TP
.BI \-\-lookback " DURATION"
.TQ
.BI \-\-lookback= DURATION
A catch-up window for the daily ingest, as a Go duration
.RB ( 168h ),
replacing
.B GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS
for this run. It is an argument rather than an environment variable because
the
.B cti\-agent
wrapper drops the environment at the sudo hop. The ingest binary refuses a
value that is not positive or is over 90 days (2160h). Ignored by the weekly.
.IP
Without it, the daily catches up by itself: when the last daily that was sent
started reading the mailbox more than 10 minutes further back than
.BR GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS ,
the run reads back to that point plus 30 minutes, at most 168h (or the
configured window, if larger), and says so. The point is saved in
.I state/daily\-read\-start
only after a real send is recorded; until the first one, the send time
.RB ( "fleet\-db last\-sent daily" )
stands in. Past the cap it reads the cap and posts an INFO line to the board;
older mail needs
.BR \-\-lookback .
The value is required: a bare
.B \-\-lookback
at the end of the line stops the runner with an unbound-variable error,
exit status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is sourced with every assignment exported, and its values replace the
process environment, except
.BR FLEET_HOME ,
.B FLEET_CODE
and
.BR FLEET_FEEDS ,
which keep the value the unit or wrapper injected.
.TP
.B FLEET_HOME
State directory: reports, state, board.
Default:\~\fI$HOME/fleet\fR; the units set \fI/var/lib/cti\-agent\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_CODE
Code directory holding
.I bin/
and
.IR lanes/ .
Default:\~\fB$FLEET_HOME\fR; the units set \fI/opt/cti\-agent\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_ENV
The config file to source.
Default:\~\fI$FLEET_HOME/fleet.env\fR; the units set
\fI/etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_FEEDS
Preserved across the source of
.I fleet.env
for the lanes; not read by the runner itself.
.TP
.B TRIAGE_ENABLED
0 or 1. 1 runs advisory triage (step 2c) in the daily; 0 or unset does not.
Any other value is off, and the log says it is not 0 or 1.
.TP
.B TRIAGE_MAX_PER_RUN
How many advisories step 2c triages. Default 10, at most 50.
.TP
.B TRIAGE_SENDERS
Senders whose authenticated mail is triaged even with no CVE, ATT&CK ID,
indicator or hash in it. Default:
.BR MAILBOX_FEED_SENDERS ;
.B none
for nobody.
.TP
.B GRAPH_MAILBOX
The CTI mailbox (section 1). The daily names it in its log, and stops with an
unbound-variable error if it is unset.
.TP
.B GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS
The ingest window when
.B \-\-lookback
is not given (section 1), read by the ingest binary.
.TP
.B CTI_AGENT_DIR
Where the ingest binary or its source lives (section 3).
.TP
.B WEEKLY_DAYS
Days of stored findings in the weekly rollup. Not in
.IR fleet.env.example .
Default:\~\fB7\fR.
.TP
.BR DIGEST_TO ", " DIGEST_CC ", " VM_ALLOW_TO
Recipients and the send allowlist, read by
.BR cti\-mailer (1)
(section 4).
.TP
.B REPORT_PATH
Set by the runner for the ingest binary; any value in the environment is
replaced.
.SH FILES
Under
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent
on the Fedora layout; files are 0600 under the units'
.BR UMask=0077 .
.I DATE
is the local date.
.TP
.I reports/raw\-DATE.md
The ingest report, 0600, attached to the mail. The weekly attaches it too
when a daily ran that day.
.TP
.I state/enriched\-DATE.json
The daily's enriched findings.
.TP
.I state/enriched\-DATE\-tickets.json
The CVE-to-ticket map
.B cti\-jira
writes for
.BR cti\-brief .
.TP
.IR state/enriched\-DATE\-advisory\-input.json ", " state/enriched\-DATE\-advisories.json
Advisory triage's input (message bodies with no CVE) and report, 0600.
Only with
.BR TRIAGE_ENABLED .
.TP
.IR state/weekly\-enriched\-DATE.json ", " state/weekly\-enriched\-DATE\-tickets.json
The weekly rollup and its borrowed ticket map, 0600. Not named
.I enriched\-*
so
.B cti\-kev
does not take the week for the day.
.TP
.IR reports/digest\-KIND\-DATE.html ", " reports/digest\-KIND\-DATE.txt
The rendered digest.
.TP
.I state/memory.db
SQLite: findings, and the digests table behind the already-sent check.
.TP
.I state/processed\-messages.json
Written by the daily's real ingest (never a dry run's), read by mailbox cleanup.
.TP
.I dryrun/digest\-KIND/
A dry run's or test send's files, replaced by the next one.
.TP
.I state/locks/digest\-KIND.lock
The lane lock.
.TP
.I board.md
The fleet board, appended to by
.BR fleet\-board .
.SH DRY RUNS AND THE LANE LOCK
A dry run and a test send
.RB ( \-\-to\-dev ,
with or without
.BR \-\-dry\-run )
write nothing a real run reads. They work under
.I $FLEET_HOME/dryrun/
(0700), emptied at the start of each one; nothing in the fleet reads it.
Its files go in
.IR dryrun/digest\-KIND/ :
the raw report, the enriched file, the digest and its own
.IR processed\-messages.json ,
so the ingest records nothing in the real processed-message log (mailbox
cleanup's authority to archive), and
.B cti\-enrich
runs with
.B \-\-no\-db
so
.I memory.db
is not written and the next real digest still flags today's CVEs as new.
It reads
.I memory.db
for those flags, and today's ticket map and
.I products.json
are copied in, so the preview shows what the real digest would.
.PP
Each run takes the lane lock,
.I state/locks/digest\-daily.lock (or \fIstate/locks/digest\-weekly.lock\fR),
with
.BR flock (2).
A dry run or test send that finds it held \- a timer run, or another
person's rehearsal \- says which process holds it and exits 75 without doing
anything; it never waits and never runs beside the real run. A real run
waits for it up to
.B FLEET_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS
(from
.IR fleet.env ,
default 600, under the unit's
.BR TimeoutStartSec ),
then, if another real run still holds it, fails with exit status 1, so the
unit fails and it can be rerun; if a rehearsal still holds it, goes ahead
with a warning, because a rehearsal writes only its own files. The
kernel drops the lock when the process ends, however it ends, so there is
no stale lock to clear.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The digest was sent, it had already been sent today, or the dry run
completed.
.TP
.B 1
A fatal step failed (FAILED and a board ERROR line), or the shell stopped
on an error outside those steps: a missing
.B \-\-lookback
value, an unset
.BR GRAPH_MAILBOX ,
or a failing subject render.
.TP
.B 2
An unknown argument.
.TP
.B 3
The digest WAS SENT, but
.B fleet\-db sent
failed, so it is not recorded and a re-run would mail a second copy. The
journal and the board ERROR line give the exact
.B fleet\-db sent
command that records it by hand; run that, not the digest. A failure to
read the counts after the send records zeros instead, and a failed closing
board post is only a warning.
.TP
.B 75
A dry run or test send refused because another run of the lane holds
the lane lock. Nothing was done; run it again when that run has finished.
.SH EXAMPLES
Rehearse today's daily, then read what it would send:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-digest daily \-\-dry\-run
sudo less /var/lib/cti\-agent/reports/digest\-daily\-$(date +%F).txt
.EE
.PP
Catch up after the timer was off for a week:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-digest daily \-\-lookback 168h
.EE
.PP
Send the Monday rollup by hand:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-digest weekly
.EE
.PP
See the last run:
.PP
.EX
journalctl \-u cti\-agent\-digest.service \-n 50
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fleet\-db (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR cti\-jira (1),
.BR cti\-kev (1),
.BR run\-mailbox\-cleanup (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
The binaries
.IR bin/cti\-enrich ,
.I bin/cti\-brief
and
.I bin/cti\-mailer
under
.IR /opt/cti\-agent .
.PP
RUNBOOK.md sections 9, 11a and 12; fleet\-kit/README.md, "Schedule".
